|term-group[ddd:big-ball-of-mud]
  :status  proposed
  :source  "Eric Evans, Domain-Driven Design Reference: Definitions and Pattern Summaries (Domain Language, 2015), CC BY 4.0 — local copy ref/DDD_Reference_2015-03/"

  A **BIG-BALL-OF-MUD** is what forms where no one has drawn @{ddd:bounded-context} boundaries, or where the choices that drew them have lapsed: several models mixed together, definitions ambiguous or contradictory, held together by logic added piece by piece. In this lexicon the term names that condition. Evans's pattern then says what to do about it: draw one boundary around the whole mess, name it, do no careful modeling inside, and watch that it does not spread. Those are practices, kept apart from what the term means, because this project often needs the condition's name without the response.

  |term[ddd:big-ball-of-mud] A part of a system where models are mixed and boundaries are inconsistent.
    :source "[SOURCE: Evans 2015, Big Ball of Mud, p. 38, modified — 'parts of systems, often large ones, where models are mixed and boundaries are inconsistent' restated as a definition]"
    |rels :bounded-by @{ddd:bounded-context} {0,1}  ; present only once someone has designated it, per the practice below
    |conditions
      - "When these factors are absent, or disappear, multiple conceptual systems and mix together, making definitions and rules ambiguous or contradictory." (p. 38; "and mix together" [sic], as in Evans's text) The factors are "intellectual choices and social forces".
    |invariants
      - Its boundaries are absent or inconsistent. Evans names both: "models are mixed and boundaries are inconsistent", and "systems where there simply are no boundaries" (p. 38).
      - It forms gradually: "The systems are made to work by contingent logic as features are added … Eventually the software congeals into a big ball of mud." (p. 38)
      - It tends to spread into other contexts: "Be alert to the tendency for such systems to sprawl into other contexts." (p. 38)
      - It can be practical: it "is actually quite practical for some situations (as described in the original article by Foote and Yoder), but it almost completely prevents the subtlety and precision needed for useful models" (p. 38).
    |practices
      - "Draw a boundary around the entire mess and designate it a big ball of mud." (p. 38)
      - "Do not try to apply sophisticated modeling within this context." (p. 38)
    |collisions
      - **Evans's designation sense.** Evans also uses the name for the designated region ("designate it a big ball of mud", p. 38). In this lexicon that is a big ball of mud with its boundary drawn, per the practice above, not a second sense of the term.

  |discussion
    - **The term can describe a self-inconsistent source; the pattern's response does not fit one.** IASR 2026 and Anthropic's August 2026 Risk Report each define some terms more than one way. Evans's prescription, to stop modeling carefully inside, is the opposite of what this project needs from them, which is precision at each place a term is used. The plan maps such sources as several scopes, one at a time (§3.2, finding 5). The term can still describe a source honestly; the designation practice would be the wrong response. — the plan's reading, kept here
    - **Model beta in the condition sense.** The plan says beta's risk analysis "began to read as its own ball of mud" (§1.2): the lexicon and the risk analysis were built together, with no boundary between them. That is the condition this term names. The gamma response is the opposite of Evans's designation practice: draw the missing boundary (lexicon first; risk analysis as a separate context built on it later, §3.3). — our reading

  |working-notes
    - The phrase is Brian Foote and Joseph Yoder's (Evans cites http://www.laputan.org/mud/mud.html). From memory, not re-read for this entry: their paper is about software architecture, while Evans's use is about model boundaries. Check before relying on the difference.
